  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1010 PM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nDeep, layered moisture continues over the CWA from the RG Valley \neast with PWs of around an inch. This could be from another upper \ntrough that rotated around the upper low over SoCal. Our round of \nsevere weather seemed to align with the back edge of this layered \nmoisture, with convection now weakened considerably and mostly over \nOtero County. \n\nSaturday and Sunday...another upper trough now over western Arizona \nwill rotate eastward Saturday, reaching the RG Valley mid afternoon. \nThis looks to be the convective initiator, limiting most of the \nthunderstorms to the valley east starting mid afternoon. MUCAPES \napproach 1000 J/kg in the east Saturday, and much like today, decent \nspeed shear with little directional shear.\nThus, some severe storms are possible again on Saturday in the east. \nSame story mostly on Sunday, though as the upper low lifts out over \nUtah and the main trough from the low moves into New Mexico, the \nconvective focus may shift even a bit further east.\nThe environment still keeps MUCAPES of around 500-1000 J/kg, but \nwith mid level winds turning more southwest, some directional shear \nis introduced back into the area. Hail likely to be the biggest \nthreat again; some flooding is possible but lower PWs and the \ncontinued rapid movement of the storms should keep this threat \nlower. \n\nMonday through Wednesday...drier air at upper levels moves in as \nupper flow turns westerly. However mid level remains southerly \nMonday so slight chance of storms remains over Hudspeth/Otero \nCounties. Tuesday and Wednesday should remain dry.\n\nThursday and Friday...mid and upper level winds become a bit more \nsouth/southwest as a Pacific trough approaches the west coast.\nThis will draw up modest amounts of moisture, so a slight chance of \nrain will also follow for both these days.",
